<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!-- The first F# assembly in the tree. It exists to answer one question: can the engine's existing
       in-process CLR host load and run F#-compiled IL at all? Everything F# is meant to do later
       (PCG generation, orchestration) is worthless if this seam does not hold, so it is proved first
       and on its own.

       HOW F# COMPILES HERE: `dotnet build` on this .fsproj, which is the same command the engine
       already runs for every C# script — modules/scripting/CMakeLists.txt at build time, and
       ToolsMenu::startCompile, which shells out to `dotnet build` on the project, at run time. MSBuild
       picks the language from the project extension, so F# needs no new compile mechanism, no second
       toolchain and no code change on the native side. The alternative, FSharp.Compiler.Service, was
       rejected on two checked grounds: this machine's NuGet cache holds exactly one F# package
       (fsharp.core) and the SDK's offline library-packs folder holds exactly one .nupkg, so FCS would
       make the first build of this tree need the network; and hosting a compiler inside a process
       whose job is to draw frames is the thing Aver.Design.csproj already argued against for Roslyn.
       Nothing about F# changes either argument.

       LICENCE: FSharp.Core is MIT. It is not a new third-party dependency either — the .NET SDK
       carries it at sdk/<version>/FSharp/library-packs/FSharp.Core.<v>.nupkg, and the SDK injects that
       folder as an extra restore source rather than as a NuGet source, so this resolves offline even
       with the repository's NuGet.config clearing every source but third_party/nuget. Measured, not
       assumed: restoring Aver.Pcg.fsproj into an empty packages folder pulls fsharp.core and nothing.

       THE COMPARISON THIS USED TO DRAW WITH ROSLYN WAS WRONG, and is corrected rather than deleted
       because it is a mistake worth being able to find. It read "exactly like Microsoft.CodeAnalysis
       does for averdesign". The SDK ships Roslyn only as the compiler's own DLLs under
       Roslyn/bincore, never as a restorable .nupkg; library-packs holds FSharp.Core and nothing else.
       Roslyn came off nuget.org the first time anyone built this tree. It is offline now only because
       it is checked in at third_party/nuget, which is a different mechanism entirely.

       NO reference to Aver.Scripting. This assembly is a pure library: the bridge's CheckApiVersion
       skips an assembly that references neither Aver.Scripting nor Aver.Framework, which is the
       documented behaviour for a support library sitting in a scripts folder. The C# behaviour next
       door is what the host discovers; this is what that behaviour calls. -->
  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFramework>net10.0</TargetFramework>
    <AssemblyName>Aver.FSharp.Sample</AssemblyName>
    <RootNamespace>Aver.FSharp.Sample</RootNamespace>
    <Version>1.0.0</Version>
    <AssemblyVersion>1.0.0.0</AssemblyVersion>
    <FileVersion>1.0.0.0</FileVersion>
    <!-- The round trip has to prove F# ran, so warnings that would let a silently-wrong function
         through are errors here. -->
    <TreatWarningsAsErrors>true</TreatWarningsAsErrors>
    <GenerateDocumentationFile>true</GenerateDocumentationFile>
    <!-- MEASURED, not assumed. The SDK defaults this to false for a LIBRARY, so a plain
         `dotnet build` of an .fsproj emits Aver.FSharp.Sample.dll with no FSharp.Core.dll beside it —
         the first build here did exactly that. The scripts folder is the host's only source for a
         script's private dependencies, so without this the assembly loads and then dies at the first
         call into it, which is the worst possible place to find out. -->
    <CopyLocalLockFileAssemblies>true</CopyLocalLockFileAssemblies>
    <!-- FSharp.Core ships thirteen localised resource assemblies. Left alone they arrive as thirteen
         subdirectories inside the scripts folder, which is a folder a user is expected to look at. -->
    <SatelliteResourceLanguages>en</SatelliteResourceLanguages>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <Compile Include="RoundTrip.fs" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
